WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.696
The year is 2006.

00:00:01.808 --> 00:00:04.022
You work in John Kuriyan's laboratory at
Berkeley.

00:00:04.621 --> 00:00:08.094
You are studying the receptor for EGF (epidermal
growth factor).

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.996
Three facts about it are known.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:18.602
1. The receptor has three parts: an outer part
that binds EGF, one pass through the membrane,
and an inner part that is a kinase.

00:00:19.203 --> 00:00:20.813
This is the kinase that Cohen found.

00:00:21.412 --> 00:00:23.161
On its own, the kinase is off.

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:26.457
2. EGF is one small protein.

00:00:27.034 --> 00:00:29.301
One EGF molecule binds one receptor.

00:00:29.518 --> 00:00:31.108
It cannot hold two receptors.

00:00:31.924 --> 00:00:34.630
3. But the receptor responds only in pairs.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:42.286
When you add EGF, receptors come together two at
a time, each with its own EGF bound, and the
kinases switch on.

00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:45.576
A receptor with EGF bound but no partner stays
off.

00:00:46.305 --> 00:00:47.369
Answer three questions.

00:00:48.209 --> 00:00:50.912
1. EGF cannot join two receptors.

00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:56.841
So what has EGF binding physically done to the
receptor, so that receptors now hold on to each
other?

00:00:57.674 --> 00:01:02.025
2. Once two receptors are held together, what
switches the kinase on?

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:04.807
Say what one kinase must do to the other.

00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:11.976
3. Predict: cut the inner kinase free from the
membrane, the outer part and EGF.

00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:16.630
Then crowd many copies of it together in a tube,
so that they touch each other.

00:01:17.186 --> 00:01:18.642
Will the kinase be on or off?

00:01:19.426 --> 00:01:21.861
Explain your prediction using your answer to
question 2.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:25.747
You now have a kinase that stays off until
another copy comes close.

00:01:26.328 --> 00:01:32.118
This page asks what the two copies do to each
other when they meet, and whether both copies
end up the same.

00:01:32.741 --> 00:01:37.465
The EGFR kinase's off shape is one that two
other enzymes also use.

00:01:38.068 --> 00:01:44.583
Src kinase and the cyclin-dependent kinases both
fold into a resting shape that blocks their own
activity.

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:49.929
The isolated EGFR kinase, left alone, has the
same kind of shape.

00:01:50.614 --> 00:01:53.275
Kuriyan's group solved the structure of the
kinase pair.

00:01:53.849 --> 00:01:58.306
In the pair, the back lobe of one kinase presses
against the front lobe of the other.

00:01:58.882 --> 00:02:00.731
So the two copies do different things.

00:02:01.269 --> 00:02:07.343
The pressing copy uses the shape of its back
surface to push the other's active site into its
working position.

00:02:07.854 --> 00:02:11.790
A cyclin protein switches on a cyclin-dependent
kinase in the same way.

00:02:12.438 --> 00:02:14.784
The copy that is pressed is the one that
switches on.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:24.026
The same contact is used across the whole family
of these receptors: EGFR, HER2, HER3 and HER4.

00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:28.781
So a cell can make different signals from the
same set of parts by pairing different members.

00:02:29.402 --> 00:02:32.726
The signal depends on which member presses and
which member is pressed.
